{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Grey (RGB) LUT}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Grey__RGB__LUT}
{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} main}
\pard\keepn \b\fs36\cf1 \{bmc bm28.bmp\}   Grey (RGB) LUT
\par \pard \plain\fs24 
\par Each point in the LUT Display Window, represents an equal Red, Green and Blue entry and thus an grey intensity value.
\par 
\par Changes in the LUT affect all three colours together.
\par 
\par When this mode is selected, it is possible also to select \uldb \b Level Mode\plain\b\fs24 {\v Level_Mode}\plain\fs24 .
\par 
\par Points can be modified by using the \uldb \b Select Mode\plain\b\fs24 {\v Select_Mode}\plain\fs24  or \uldb \b Add/Delete Point\plain\b\fs24 {\v Add_Delete_Point}\plain\fs24  functions.
\par 
\par Each of the Red, Green and Blue LUT components can however be edited separately, as each has a dedicated LUT associated with it. To access these LUTs, one of the \uldb \b Red LUT\plain\b\fs24 {\v Red_LUT}\plain\fs24 , \uldb \b Green LUT\plain\b\fs24 {\v Green_LUT}\plain\fs24  or \uldb \b Blue LUT\plain\b\fs24 {\v Blue_LUT}\plain\fs24  modes has first to be selected.
\par \pard 
\par \{bmc bm36.bmp\}
\par \page
