{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} LUT Editor File Types}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} G3GFNS}
{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} main}
\pard\keepn\tx355 \b\fs36\cf1 LUT Editor File Types
\par \pard\tx355 \plain\fs24 
\par \pard\tx355 There are 3 different types of file associated with the LUT Editor. 
\par \pard\tx355 
\par \pard\tx355 There are two formats for the Display LUT and one format for the Input LUT:
\par \pard\tx355 
\par \pard\tx355 \fs28 Input LUT Files (.ULU)
\par \pard\tx355 \fs24 
\par \pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 \fs28 \tab \fs24 Input LUT files have the extension\b  .ULU\plain\fs24 . The current working file will be called \b CURRENT.ULU\plain\fs24 . Files saved in this format can only be used when the LUT Editor is in Input LUT mode.\fs28 
\par \pard\tx355 \fs24 
\par \pard\tx355 \fs28 Display LUT Files (.OLT, .DLU)
\par \pard\tx355 \fs24 
\par \pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 \fs28 \tab \fs24 The reason for the display LUT having two file formats is that if the user wishes to load in a \i special\plain\fs24  user defined LUT, then the definition for the LUT can be saved in ASCII format and edited by the user in a text editor such as \i Notepad\plain\fs24 .
\par 
\par \tab \b .OLT\plain\fs24  files are in a binary format and cannot be modified by the user.
\par 
\par \tab \b .DLU\plain\fs24  files are in ASCII format and can be modified by the user. See \uldb \b Sample .DLU File\plain\b\fs24 {\v Sample_.DLU_File}\plain\fs28 
\par \page
