{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Look Up Table Editor}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Look_Up_Table_Editor}
{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} main}
\pard\keepn {\up K}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up K} Clour Selection Panel;Colour Selection;Contents;Contents;Display LUT;Edit Mode;Functions List;Gamma;Green LUT}
\f1\b\fs36\cf1 Look Up Table Editor
\par \pard \plain\fs24 
\par \b Input Look Up Table\plain\b\fs24 {\v 1QUPBH3@SEM.HLP}\plain\fs24  and \b Display Look Up Table\plain\fs24  can be loaded, edited, and saved with the \uldb \b Look Up Table Editor Panel\plain\b\fs24 {\v Look_Up_Table_Editor_Panel}.\plain\fs24 
\par 
\par \b\fs28 Input LUT:
\par \plain\fs24 The \uldb \b Input LUT\plain\b\fs24 {\v Input_LUT}\plain\fs24  is used to perform a translation on the input signal as defined by the pattern loaded into the LUT.
\par 
\par The pattern may be transparent (no transformation), a gamma transformation or a user defined pattern created in the Input LUT Editor Window.
\par 
\par The pattern is defined as points which may be manipulated using the add, move and delete functions.
\par \pard 
\par \b\fs28 Display LUT:
\par \plain\fs24 
\par The \uldb \b Display LUT\plain\b\fs24 {\v Display_LUT}\plain\fs24  translates the \ul pixel\plain\fs24 {\v PIXEL@Leo_1500.HLP} values in the image store into Red, Green and Blue signals for the display monitor.
\par 
\par The window represents the grey level in the X direction and the monitor gun intensity in the Y direction. The window Y size is determined by the scale function.
\par 
\par There are several modes of operation which affect the Display LUT: Stereo, X-Ray dot mapping and user defined. 
\par This window of the LUT Editor controls the user defined setting.
\par \pard 
\par In this window the LUT may be used in grey or in colour modes.
\par 
\par For test purposes a grey wedge may be generated.
\par 
\par \b Setup of the LUTs\plain\fs24  is done through the \uldb \b LUT Editor Window\plain\b\fs24 {\v LUT_Editor_Window}\plain\fs24 
\par 
\par For additional information about signal routing in the instrument see \uldb \b Signal Path\plain\b\fs24 {\v M6O594@SEM.HLP}\plain\fs24 
\par 
\par \page
