\pard\keepn\tx355 \b\fs36\cf1 Pseudo Colour Display
\par \pard\tx355 \plain\fs24 
\par \pard\tx355 This feature allows a LUT to be easily divided up into colour bands for example to highlight chosen ranges of pixel values.
\par \pard\tx355 
\par \trowd\trgaph105\trleft-116 \cellx5095\cellx8995\cellx13755\pard\intbl\li355\fi-355\tx355 \b \{bmc bm9.bmp\}\plain\fs24 \tab Select \uldb \b Step LUT\plain\b\fs24 {\v Step_LUT}\plain\fs24  and set the number of grey levels (steps)
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl \b \{bmc bm9.bmp\}\plain\fs24 \tab Use \uldb \b Select Mode\plain\b\fs24 {\v Select_Mode}\plain\fs24  to equalise the steps.
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl \b \{bmc bm9.bmp\}\plain\fs24 \tab Adjust a band of grey (in the example in the white region) and double \ul click\plain\fs24 {\v CLICK@Sem.HLP} on it to open the \uldb \b Colour Selection Panel\plain\b\fs24 {\v Colour_Selection_Panel}
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl \{bmc bm9.bmp\}\tab \plain\fs24 Select the color (Red in the example). The image is displayed in pseudo colour.
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl \b \{bmc bm9.bmp\}\plain\fs24 \tab Save the LUT with \uldb \b Save LUT As\plain\b\fs24 {\v Save_LUT_As}\plain\fs24 \cell\pard \pard\intbl\li355\fi-355\tx355 \pard\intbl \{bmc bm58.bmp\}\cell\pard \pard\intbl 
