\b\fs36\cf1 What is the LUT Editor
\par \pard \plain\fs24 
\par The LEO-32 LUT Editor is a pop-up application or applet that allows the SEM operator to modify either the Input or Display \ul Look Up Tables\plain\fs24 {\v 1QUPBH3@Sem.HLP} (LUTs). These LUTs allow the user to highlight specific areas of interest on the specimen by modifying the colour values (grey values only for Input LUTs) used to represent the stored image data.
\par 
\par Both the input and display LUTs are described as a set of points that define the profile of the selected LUT in graphical format. The X-axis of a LUT represents the LUT colour entry in ascending order. The Y-axis of the LUT represents the intensity of the colour entry from minimum to maximum.
\par \pard 
\par As each LUT is described by a set of points, the lines between adjacent points define a gradient of intensity for the range of LUT entries between these adjacent points. Thus for a linear LUT, it is only necessary to have two points one at (Xmin,Ymin) and the other at (Xmax, Ymax), which define a 1:1 mapping between LUT entries and intensity.
\par 
\par The LUT Editor is controlled mainly by a simple 'selection and execution' process using the mouse. The user 'selects' a toolbar function and 'executes' its features by \ul clicking\plain\fs24 {\v CLICK@Sem.HLP} and \ul dragging\plain\fs24 {\v DRAG@Sem.HLP} the LUT points.
\par \pard 
\par All changes with the exception of the Step LUT function that are made to the Input or Display LUTs are applied immediately to the image store. After each operation, an Undo feature is available that restores the last set of LUT values.
\par \page
